**`src/agents/tier1/memory-agent.ts:413-452` — `consolidateMemory` is O(n²) plus per-match individual fetches**
```ts
for (const entry of allEntries) {                       // O(n)
  const similar = await this.vectorStore.search(entry.embedding, 5, threshold); // O(n) linear scan itself
  for (const s of toMerge) {
    const similarEntry = await this.vectorStore.retrieve(s.id);   // separate lookup per match
    ...
  }
}
```
`search()` already does a full linear scan per call (see #5), so this function is effectively O(n²) — for 10k memory entries that's ~100M cosine-similarity ops per consolidation pass, run on a periodic timer (`memory-agent.ts:540`). Fix: batch-retrieve matched IDs in one pass (`vectorStore.getMany(ids)`), and only run consolidation over entries changed since the last pass.

**`src/agents/tier2/risk-detection-agent.ts:207-211` — sequential await-in-loop over campaigns**
```ts
for (const campaign of campaigns) {
  const campaignAlerts = await this.analyzeSingleCampaign(campaign, context); // serialized
  ...
}
```
If `analyzeSingleCampaign` ever calls out to a real data source (BigQuery, ad platform API), this becomes classic N+1 — one round trip per campaign instead of a batched query. Fix:
```ts
const results = await Promise.all(campaigns.map(c => this.analyzeSingleCampaign(c, context)));
```

**Forward-looking**: `src/integrations/gcp/bigquery-client.ts` is currently a no-op mock. When it's wired to real BigQuery, watch every place that loops calling `.query()` per entity (campaigns, creatives) — use `WHERE id IN UNNEST(@ids)` batched queries, not per-ID calls.

**`src/core/state-manager.ts:377-387` + `getState()` (100-110)**
```ts
private notifySubscribers(): void {
  const stateCopy = this.getState();   // deep-clones ALL 4 Maps (agents, tasks, campaigns, creatives)
  this.subscribers.forEach((callback) => callback(stateCopy));
}
```
This runs on **every single** `updateAgent`, `updateTask`, `addTask`, `updateCampaign`, etc. — and `BaseAgent.submitTask` (`base-agent.ts`) calls `stateManager.updateTask` multiple times per task (start, complete). With N tasks and M entities in state, you get O(N × M) clone work over a session — the same shape of problem as a React context re-rendering every subscriber on every keystroke. Fixes:
- Only clone the Map that actually changed, not all four.
- Debounce/batch notifications (e.g. via microtask) instead of firing synchronously per mutation.
- Let subscribers register for specific slices (`subscribeToTasks`, `subscribeToAgents`) instead of full-state callbacks.

**`src/performance/cache.ts` builds `LRUCache`/`TieredCache`/`getCampaignCache()`/`getAnalyticsCache()` — but nothing in `src/services/*` actually calls them.**

- `AnalyticsService.generateCampaignReport()` (`analytics-service.ts:99-123`) recomputes `analyzeTrends` + `generateCampaignInsights` from scratch on every call, even though `getAnalyticsCache()` exists unused.
- `CampaignService.getOptimizationRecommendations()` (`campaign-service.ts:228-275`) does the same — no memoization despite being read-heavy (likely called on every dashboard refresh).

Example fix:
```ts
async generateCampaignReport(campaignId: string): Promise<PerformanceReport> {
  return getAnalyticsCache().getOrSet(
    `report:${campaignId}`,
    async () => this.buildCampaignReport(campaignId),
    30_000 // 30s TTL — reports don't need to be recomputed on every request
  );
}
```
Invalidate on the relevant event (`campaign.updated`, `analytics.metrics_recorded`) rather than relying purely on TTL.

**`src/core/event-bus.ts:279-284` — `eventStore` (per-aggregate event history) is never trimmed.**
```ts
private storeByAggregate(event: DomainEvent): void {
  const events = this.eventStore.get(aggregateId) ?? [];
  events.push(event);
  this.eventStore.set(aggregateId, events);   // grows forever, no cap, no expiry
}
```
Unlike `eventHistory` (capped at `maxEventHistory: 10000`, `event-bus.ts:271`), `eventStore` has no size limit per aggregate or eviction of old aggregate IDs. In a long-running process handling many campaigns/tasks, this is an unbounded Map — a genuine memory leak. Fix: cap per-aggregate array length and evict LRU aggregate IDs, or move to the existing `LRUCache`.

**`src/agents/tier1/orchestrator-agent.ts:203-205` and `src/agents/tier2/risk-detection-agent.ts:637-639`** already correctly trim (`routingHistory`, `alertHistory` capped at 1000). Good pattern — just needs to be applied consistently to `eventStore` and to `StateManager.snapshots` if `startAutoSnapshot()` runs indefinitely (it's capped via `maxSnapshots`, so that one's fine — but each snapshot is a full deep clone per `createSnapshot()`, which combined with the 60s default interval means one O(n) clone every minute for the life of the process, compounding with #2).

**`src/performance/cache.ts:198-205`** — `cleanupTimer.unref()` is good practice (doesn't block process exit), but there's no equivalent for `ConnectionPool`'s `idleCheckTimer` in terms of being stoppable if `drain()` is never called on shutdown — worth confirming callers always call `.drain()`/`.stop()`.

**`src/agents/tier1/memory-agent.ts` `VectorStore.search()` and `src/agents/tier2/historical-memory-agent.ts` `HistoricalStore` — brute-force O(n) cosine similarity despite doc comments claiming "HNSW-powered similarity search".**
```ts
async search(query: number[], limit = 10, minScore = 0.5): Promise<SimilarityResult[]> {
  for (const [id, embedding] of this.embeddings) {   // scans every stored vector, every call
    const score = this.cosineSimilarity(query, embedding);
    ...
  }
}
```
This recomputes full similarity against every entry on every search — no actual index. Given CLAUDE.md/docs advertise HNSW-based 150x speedups elsewhere in this project, this mock implementation is the actual bottleneck once entry counts grow past a few thousand. If a real HNSW library (e.g. via the referenced agentdb/ruvector integration) isn't wired in yet, that's the highest-leverage fix in this codebase.

**`src/services/analytics-service.ts:76-85` `recordMetrics`** filters the entire history array by a 90-day cutoff on every single write:
```ts
recordMetrics(entityId: string, metrics: MetricSnapshot): void {
  const history = this.metricHistory.get(entityId) || [];
  history.push(metrics);
  const filtered = history.filter((m) => m.timestamp >= cutoff); // O(n) every write
  this.metricHistory.set(entityId, filtered);
}
```
For frequent metric writes (e.g. per-hour snapshots × many campaigns), this is a full array scan per insert. Fix: only run the cutoff-trim periodically (e.g. every Nth write or on a timer) rather than on every single `recordMetrics` call, since a few stale entries lingering briefly is harmless.

**`src/services/analytics-service.ts:301-340` `analyzeTrends`** recomputes `history.map(...)` three separate times per metric (`calculateTrend` called 5×, each doing its own `.map()` over the same `history` array) — could compute all 5 metric arrays in a single pass instead of 5 independent `.map()` traversals, though at typical history sizes (≤90 entries) this is minor compared to the two issues above.

---

**Priority order if you want to fix a few things first:**
1. `event-bus.ts` `eventStore` unbounded growth (real leak)
2. `state-manager.ts` full-clone-per-mutation (compounds with everything else)
3. Wire an actual vector index into `VectorStore`/`HistoricalStore` before entry counts grow
4. Hook the existing `LRUCache` into `AnalyticsService`/`CampaignService` (infrastructure already built, just unused)
5. Parallelize the `risk-detection-agent.ts` campaign scan loop

